PASS level_intents: all 66 levels have a specific intent
PASS no_filler_levels: no trivial filler levels after the tutorial
FAIL level_design: level-design review 6.5/10 (bar 8), 12 level(s) below 6/10: Strong design skeleton: one idea per teach unit, transparent READ numbers, and sound ordering (teach, twist, combine, spike, breather, boss). The recurring issues are these. (1) Difficulty and length grow too fast. From Ch2 many units have 51-65% casual win rates, and the bosses (L22, L33, L40, L44, L55, L66) have 51-63% mid-skill rates over 15-21 raids. That is a churn cliff. (2) Several units duplicate earlier ones: L2/L3/L5, L23/L43, L26/L47, L37/L59, L45/L54, L35/L36. (3) Some 'teach' units are blocked by hard goals (L46, L49, L56). (4) Some READ numbers make one option dominate (Dash in L3, Toe in L12, Dubki in L24 and L48), so the choice is no choice. (5) The auction, the genre's retention engine, appears in only four units and has little consequence afterwards. (6) The set offers little to bring players back: no star-gated replays, no upgrade payoff visible in the trials, no season variety. Fix by trimming bosses to about 15 raids, lowering the grip on spikes, merging duplicate units, and linking squad upgrades to later matches. Curve: Strong skeleton: six chapters with one idea per teach unit, then a combine, a spike, a breather and a boss. Each new verb is introduced alone (walk, then Dash, Toe, Dubki, bonus, Kick, lunge, Rush, Super, chain), and the READ rows give honest numbers. The weaknesses are these. (1) Difficulty is front-loaded at the start and then runs flat. Ch1 is gentle (80-99% wins). From Ch2 the casual win rate falls to 55-70%, and the bosses end at 51-63% for mid-skill players. These bosses are long (17-21 raids) with 30-40% skilled loss rates. A retention funnel of this kind will bleed players at L17, L22, L40, L44, L49, L52, L55 and L66. (2) Pacing in Ch1 is too slow. 11 trials teach hand touch, walk and thigh-holders. L1-L3, L5 and L7 all reuse the same 'rookie vs T gap' read, so the Ch1 puzzle count could be cut to about 8. (3) The runs of teach-defend units in Ch4 (L35, L36, L37, L39) are too similar. They are four defend-stand drills with the same tap-the-matching-hold decision, and they bunch together. They should be interleaved with raids and matches. (4) Auction content is thin and stands apart from the rest. It is the genre's main hook, but only 3 Ch4 units are auction (L34, L41, L42) plus L44 as an optional one, and the auction has no persistent consequence visible in later trials. Ch5-6 give no further squad-building, and the squad is the retention engine in the benchmark games (FC Mobile, Top Eleven). (5) Mid-skill players meet a difficulty wall from L40 on. Several units show 'competent' wins of 29-34/40 (72-85%) but 'mid-skill' wins of 54-70%, a gap too wide to be explained by skill. They need either an assist or a difficulty option. (6) Ch5-6 introduce fatigue, rotation, rival captains and themes, which are good. But the final three chapters stack three mechanics at once (L66: captain, feint-tell, fatigue), so the finale tests memory and not mastery. (7) The meta loop (upgrades, Kit Album, ladder, season cups) has no content tied to it in this set. The set needs star-gated replays and daily ghost variants to give a reason to come back. Overall the progression is smart in structure but too hard in the back half and too light on squad-building.
REDESIGN THESE LEVELS (keep the ones not listed):
L49 (4/10): Competent 29/40 and best 2 stars; 5 points in 5 raids with held rates 30-40% is unfair for a twist, and combines five verbs. → Goal 4 points; make T corners lighter.
L66 (4/10): Casual 51%, mid 54%; 0-2 start, win by 2 across 21 raids with the hardest mat (grip 1.62, walk/dash/dubki 52-91% held). Tests endurance and luck, not mastery. → Win by 1, grip 1.3, cut to 15 raids, one feature stack.
L17 (5/10): Win 66% casual, mid 68%; with 2 raids and a 3-point goal the solution is forced and fragile. Held gems at 2-9% make it look trivial while the goal needs a greedy second touch the text says is risky; READ numbers and goal contradict. → Lower goal to 2 points with 3-star at 3, or give 3 raids. Make the Do-or-Die the goal, not an extra constraint.
L22 (5/10): Mid-skill 61%; competent 34/40; 21 raids is long for a session; stats like walk 75% held show a mat that is mostly unwinnable at the skill level taught. → Cut to ~15 raids and drop the grip 1.13 to ~0.9.
L40 (5/10): Casual 55%, mid 67%; every mat read at once (A/T/B/R) with 5 raids; dense and punishing with a skill gap, and the strongest mat at 'grip 0.90' when the player has a built squad. → Drop to 4 raids and win-by-1, or reduce one defender type.
L44 (5/10): Optional auction is no real gate; mid 63%, 17 raids with grip 1.38. → Make auction buys modify the match (stats) and drop the grip.
L46 (5/10): Competent 27/40, casual 59%, the lowest for a Defend teach. 'Chain 2 in 5 raids' means a 70%-ish roll twice. → Goal 1 chain in 5 raids; raise the chain window.
L47 (5/10): Casual 59%, concede max 1 means one failed tackle ends it; feels punishing rather than interesting. Duplicates L26. → Allow 2 conceded and differentiate from L26 via chain.
L52 (5/10): Mid 60%, casual 63%, scouted/feint/2-touch all stacked. → Remove feints.
L56 (5/10): Win by 4 over 6 raids against a grip 1.10 mat; the lesson is about rotation yet the target is a blowout; competent 30/40. → Win by 2.
L64 (5/10): Casual 56%, mid 55%; 24-25 down with 3 on the mat vs a Hard CPU, with held rates of 70%+ on the mat. → Add 1 revival.
L65 (5/10): 21 raids; a 2-in-3 kick plus fatigue and feints; casual 63%. → Cut to 15 raids.
L2 (6/10): Near-duplicate of L1 with a rookie pair; the choice is one obvious safe pair. Skilled 91% < casual 93% is odd. → Merge with L3's idea or make the gap move per raid so players must re-read.
L3 (6/10): Dash shown at 8% vs walk 20% means Dash simply dominates; no trade-off, so not a decision. The 'aha' is a free upgrade. → Make Dash cost something (e.g. a higher rate vs rookies or a stamina cost) so walk vs Dash is a real choice.
L6 (6/10): Interesting twist, but the 82/100/55 ring tells the answer. Hold choice is fixed, so it reduces to 'tap the neighbour'. → Randomise the raider's move set (hand/toe) so the hold choice and cover choice both matter.
L7 (6/10): Struggle is the headline mini-game, yet it is taught inside a puzzle where the optimal play is to avoid it. Competent loss 5/40. → Start the raid already held on raid 1 so everyone plays the struggle once.
L19 (6/10): Dense text for a tiny lesson; 'forced raider is predictable' is not really demonstrated because he still mixes hand/toe. → Make the forced raider throw one fixed move per raid, telegraph it, and show the revival animation.
L21 (6/10): The first ghost duel is in the 'breather' slot but needs a score chase of 4 with 33/40 wins - not a breather. It also introduces the flagship mode with no async ladder context. → Lower the ghost to 3, show the ghost replay first, and flag it as the gateway to the Ghost Ladder.
L27 (6/10): Casual 73%, mid 71%; the lesson 'alternate moves' is simple, but the ~7 point swing is minor against base rates of 40%+, so scouting barely matters. → Raise the scouting effect or show a clear bar.
L33 (6/10): Mid 61%, 15 raids, with two stacked read challenges; the 'win casual 71%' is brittle. → Lower start deficit to 1 point or reduce grip.
L35 (6/10): Competent loss 10/40 on a teach; the specialist is only 1.25x so the gain is small and hard to feel. → Raise the multiplier visibly and show a glow on the matching specialist.
L45 (6/10): Goal is at the edge of the raid clock (21 of 30 s) and held rates are high (47% on T). Casual 64%. → Open on a soft mat.
L50 (6/10): Another score puzzle where DRS is a side resource; the wrong-call lose condition is a luck cliff. → Give a forgiving 3-of-4 goal.
L53 (6/10): Casual 51%; beat-5 in 5 raids on a Cage is harsh. → Set the ghost at 4.
L54 (6/10): Duplicate of L45 on a soft mat. → Add a spectacle: slow-mo camera.

level-design score 6.5/10 — Strong design skeleton: one idea per teach unit, transparent READ numbers, and sound ordering (teach, twist, combine, spike, breather, boss). The recurring issues are these. (1) Difficulty and length grow too fast. From Ch2 many units have 51-65% casual win rates, and the bosses (L22, L33, L40, L44, L55, L66) have 51-63% mid-skill rates over 15-21 raids. That is a churn cliff. (2) Several units duplicate earlier ones: L2/L3/L5, L23/L43, L26/L47, L37/L59, L45/L54, L35/L36. (3) Some 'teach' units are blocked by hard goals (L46, L49, L56). (4) Some READ numbers make one option dominate (Dash in L3, Toe in L12, Dubki in L24 and L48), so the choice is no choice. (5) The auction, the genre's retention engine, appears in only four units and has little consequence afterwards. (6) The set offers little to bring players back: no star-gated replays, no upgrade payoff visible in the trials, no season variety. Fix by trimming bosses to about 15 raids, lowering the grip on spikes, merging duplicate units, and linking squad upgrades to later matches.
